# Complete Project Steps

## Step 1 — Create the Java Application

First, create your Java application.

Example:

```text
Java Application
       ↓
Spring Boot
       ↓
Maven
       ↓
pom.xml
```

Your project contains:

```text
project/
├── src/
│   ├── main/
│   │   └── java/
│   └── test/
├── pom.xml
└── ...
```

### Purpose

This is the actual application that you are going to build and deploy.

---

# Step 2 — Create GitHub Repository

Create a repository in GitHub.

Example:

```text
Java-Application
```

Then upload your project:

```bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin <repository-url>
git push -u origin main
```

Now your source code is stored in GitHub.

```text
Developer
    ↓
Git
    ↓
GitHub Repository
```

---

# Step 3 — Create GitHub Actions Workflow

Inside your repository create:

```text
.github/
└── workflows/
    └── ci-cd.yml
```

This YAML file defines your pipeline.

For example:

```yaml
name: Java CI/CD
```

When you push code:

```text
git push
    ↓
GitHub
    ↓
GitHub Actions
    ↓
Pipeline starts
```

---

# Step 4 — Run Pipeline on GitHub Shared Runner

Initially you can use:

```yaml
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
```

This means GitHub provides the machine for your job.

Example:

```yaml
build:
  runs-on: ubuntu-latest
```

Then:

```yaml
- uses: actions/checkout@v4
```

downloads your GitHub repository onto the runner.

Then install Java:

```yaml
- name: Set up JDK 17
  uses: actions/setup-java@v4
  with:
    java-version: '17'
    distribution: 'temurin'
    cache: maven
```

Then build:

```yaml
- name: Build
  run: mvn clean compile
```

### Flow

```text
GitHub
   ↓
Shared Runner
   ↓
Checkout Code
   ↓
Install Java
   ↓
Maven Build
```

---

# Step 5 — Configure AWS EC2 as Private Runner

Instead of GitHub's machine, you create an AWS EC2 Ubuntu machine.

Install the required tools:

```text
EC2 Ubuntu
│
├── Git
├── Java
├── Maven
├── Docker
└── GitHub Actions Runner
```

Then download the GitHub Actions runner from:

```text
GitHub Repository
→ Settings
→ Actions
→ Runners
→ New self-hosted runner
```

Follow the commands provided by GitHub.

Your runner directory might be:

```bash
~/actions-runner
```

Start it:

```bash
cd ~/actions-runner
./run.sh
```

You should see:

```text
Connected to GitHub
Listening for Jobs
```

Then change:

```yaml
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
```

to:

```yaml
runs-on: self-hosted
```

Now your pipeline executes on your own EC2 machine.

---

# Step 6 — Set Up SonarQube Server

You install SonarQube on the AWS machine.

You used Docker for this:

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name sonar \
  -p 9000:9000 \
  sonarqube:lts-community
```

Then check:

```bash
docker ps
```

SonarQube is accessed through:

```text
http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP:9000
```

You then create a SonarQube project.

Example:

```text
Project Key:
Boardgame
```

For your actual application, use your own project name/key.

---

# Step 7 — Create SonarQube Token

Inside SonarQube:

```text
My Account
   ↓
Security
   ↓
Generate Token
```

Copy the token.

Then in GitHub:

```text
Repository
→ Settings
→ Secrets and variables
→ Actions
```

Create:

```text
SONAR_TOKEN
```

as a **Secret**.

Also create:

```text
SONAR_HOST_URL
```

as a **Variable**.

Example:

```text
SONAR_HOST_URL=http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP:9000
```

---

# Step 8 — Add SonarQube Job to Pipeline

Now your pipeline can perform code-quality checking.

For Maven, you can use the Maven Sonar goal:

```bash
mvn clean verify sonar:sonar \
  -Dsonar.host.url="${SONAR_HOST_URL}" \
  -Dsonar.token="${SONAR_TOKEN}"
```

The process becomes:

```text
Java Code
    ↓
Maven Build
    ↓
SonarQube
    ↓
Code Quality Analysis
    ↓
Quality Gate
```

SonarQube checks things such as:

```text
Bugs
Vulnerabilities
Code Smells
Code Quality
Test Coverage
```

---

# Step 9 — Add Security Checking

Your project also has security checks.

### Trivy

Trivy checks for known vulnerabilities.

```bash
trivy fs --scanners vuln .
```

### Gitleaks

Gitleaks checks whether secrets were accidentally committed:

```bash
gitleaks detect --source .
```

So your pipeline becomes:

```text
Build
  ↓
Trivy
  ↓
Gitleaks
  ↓
Unit Tests
  ↓
SonarQube
```

