# Step 10 — Set Up Docker Job in Pipeline

This is the step you specifically mentioned at **00:59:20**.

Now you take your Java application and create a Docker image.

First create a `Dockerfile`.

Example:

```dockerfile
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre

WORKDIR /app

COPY target/*.jar app.jar

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
```

The Maven build produces:

```text
target/
   └── application.jar
```

Docker copies that JAR into the image.

### Pipeline flow

```text
Java Source Code
       ↓
Maven
       ↓
JAR file
       ↓
Dockerfile
       ↓
Docker Build
       ↓
Docker Image
```

Example:

```bash
docker build -t my-java-app .
```

Check:

```bash
docker images
```

---

# Step 11 — Push Docker Image to Container Registry

You need somewhere to store your Docker image.

For AWS, use:

### Amazon ECR

The flow becomes:

```text
GitHub Actions
      ↓
Docker Build
      ↓
Docker Image
      ↓
Amazon ECR
```

Example image:

```text
123456789012.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/my-java-app:latest
```

Your Kubernetes cluster will later pull this image from ECR.

---

# Step 12 — Create EKS Kubernetes Cluster

This is the next section you mentioned at **01:04:06**.

Now you create your Kubernetes cluster using:

### Amazon EKS

Architecture:

```text
AWS
 │
 └── EKS Cluster
       │
       ├── Control Plane
       │
       └── Worker Nodes
```

The worker nodes are EC2 machines managed as part of the EKS cluster.

Your application will eventually run inside these nodes as Kubernetes Pods.

---

# Step 13 — Configure kubectl

You need `kubectl` to communicate with your EKS cluster.

After the EKS cluster is created, configure your local/runner environment:

```bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig \
  --region <region> \
  --name <cluster-name>
```

Then test:

```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

You should see your EKS worker nodes.

Example:

```text
NAME                           STATUS
ip-10-0-1-100.ec2.internal     Ready
ip-10-0-2-100.ec2.internal     Ready
```

Now:

```text
kubectl
   ↓
EKS Cluster
```

---

# Step 14 — Create Kubernetes Deployment

Create:

```text
deployment.yaml
```

Example structure:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: java-app

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: java-app

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: java-app

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: java-app
          image: <ECR-IMAGE>
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080
```

This tells Kubernetes:

> Run two copies of my application.

So:

```text
Deployment
     ↓
ReplicaSet
     ↓
┌─────────┐
│ Pod 1   │
└─────────┘

┌─────────┐
│ Pod 2   │
└─────────┘
```

---

# Step 15 — Create Kubernetes Service

Create:

```text
service.yaml
```

The Service allows users to access the application.

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service

metadata:
  name: java-app-service

spec:
  type: LoadBalancer

  selector:
    app: java-app

  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
```

Flow:

```text
Internet
   ↓
AWS Load Balancer
   ↓
Kubernetes Service
   ↓
┌───────┬───────┐
↓       ↓       ↓
Pod 1   Pod 2   Pod 3
```

---

# Step 16 — Kubernetes Job in GitHub Actions

Now the important final step.

Instead of manually doing:

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
```

you put these commands into GitHub Actions.

For example:

```yaml
- name: Deploy to EKS
  run: |
    kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
    kubectl apply -f service.yaml
```

Now GitHub Actions automatically deploys your new Docker image to EKS.

